      learningGoals: ["Implement compareTo so a type carries its own natural ordering", "Sort with Comparator.comparing and change direction with reversed", "Explain the contract compareTo must satisfy: negative, zero, positive", "Say why List.sort is stable and what that preserves"],
      explanation: "Sorting needs one question answered: which of these two values comes first? Java offers two places to answer it. Comparable is the ordering a type has by itself, so a class that implements it can be sorted by Collections.sort or List.sort with no extra argument, and the ordering travels with the data. Comparator is an ordering supplied from outside, which is what makes it possible to sort the same type by length, by name, or by any other rule without touching the type; Comparator.comparing builds one from a key, and reversed turns it around. Both paths rest on the same contract: a comparison returns a negative number when the first value comes first, zero when they are equivalent for ordering, and a positive number when the second comes first. Violating that contract, for example by returning zero for values the caller expects to be ordered, produces results that look arbitrary because the sorting algorithm is entitled to assume consistency. Java's List.sort is stable, so values that compare equal keep their relative order from the input, which is the property that lets a second key refine an earlier sort instead of destroying it. There is no JDK in this sandbox, so every expected value below was derived by reading the code against the API contract.",
      keywordNotes: ["Comparable is the type's own natural ordering, and compareTo is the method that expresses it.", "Comparator is an ordering supplied from outside, so one type can have many orderings.", "Comparator.comparing extracts a key, and reversed flips the resulting order.", "compareTo must return negative, zero, or positive consistently, matching how equals treats the same values.", "List.sort is stable, so equal elements keep their input order."],
